This project drafts articles that are kept in the GitHub repository `{{repo}}` (branch `{{branch}}`).

Before anything else, read `AGENTS.md` at the root of that repository and follow it. It sets the file names, the branch and pull request for every round, the commit messages, how to read a review, when to merge and what never to do. If you can read repository files with a tool (GitHub's `get_file_contents`), fetch `AGENTS.md` first. If you can only see this project's knowledge, say so and ask for `AGENTS.md` to be added to it.

The publication's own voice and editorial rules are in `instructions/publication.md` in the same repository. Read that too when it exists.

The project's built-in GitHub integration syncs only `{{branch}}`, which holds accepted text. Its knowledge should hold `AGENTS.md`, the `templates/` folder, `instructions/publication.md` and, if you like, the accepted `draft.md` and `metadata.md` of the article being worked on. Never add an article's `pipeline/` folder or the whole repository: knowledge that outgrows the context window switches to search, and a rule is then found only if a search happens to hit it. Use "Sync now" after a round is merged.

A round in progress is a branch and a pull request, and the integration cannot see either. Every round needs the GitHub connector (its tools are named in `AGENTS.md`): create the branch, commit the draft and metadata, open the pull request, read the review, commit the revision. Without the connector, say so and ask the author to do the round by hand on github.com.
